+ * choice (docs/mercury-implementace.md §6.6 decision 5, "strop módu"). Ranks
+ * per arq_fsm.c's mode_rank(): 0=DATAC15, 1=DATAC4, 2=DATAC3, 3=DATAC1,
+ * 4=DATAC17, 5=QAM16C2. Only gates what WE propose to step UP to in
+ * select_best_mode()/clamp_payload_mode_to_bandwidth() — it does not touch
+ * MODE_REQ acceptance (arq_fsm.c's ARQ_EV_RX_MODE_REQ handling), because that
+ * path only ever updates peer_tx_mode to track what the PEER is sending for
+ * our own RX demodulator to follow; it never asks us to change our own TX
+ * mode, so there is nothing for a local ceiling to refuse there.
+ *
+ * Default is UNLIMITED (ARQ_LADDER_LEVELS - 1) — this is shared code, linked
+ * into native `mercury`/its own test/sim suite as well as WIFILT's WASM
+ * build, and native has never had a ceiling concept at all. A DATAC3 default
+ * here once broke test_sim_fade_cliff_downgrades: capping the shared engine
+ * meant the sim's "good phase" could no longer climb past DATAC3, leaving a
+ * larger backlog than the fade-floor phase's time budget could clear --
+ * exposing that this default is genuinely upstream-facing, not
+ * WIFILT-browser-facing. WIFILT's OWN default (DATAC3, matching what the
+ * WASM build could reach before this setting existed --
+ * host-stubs.c's own arq_bandwidth_allows_mode() used to hard-block DATAC1)
+ * lives entirely at the JS layer instead: mercury-tuning.js's own
+ * DEFAULTS.modeCeiling, applied unconditionally by mercury-worker.js at the
+ * start of every session. DATAC17/QAM16C2 stay hard-blocked by
+ * arq_bandwidth_allows_mode() regardless of this ceiling in the WASM build
+ * specifically (its freedv modem has neither compiled in -- see
+ * build-worker-wasm.sh's own -DFREEDV_MODE_*_EN flags) -- unrelated to this
+ * default, which native code paths without that restriction are free to use
+ * in full. */
+#define ARQ_MODE_CEILING_RANK_DEFAULT  (ARQ_LADDER_LEVELS - 1)
+extern _Atomic int arq_mode_ceiling_rank;
+#define ARQ_MODE_CEILING_RANK  atomic_load(&arq_mode_ceiling_rank)
+
